---
import ModellingLayout from '@/layouts/ModellingLayout.astro';
import ModellingVisual from '@/components/modelling/ModellingVisual.astro';
---

<ModellingLayout title="What happens to a contribution?" description="Discussion, a proposed change, technical checks and adoption are different events. Follow the Harbour Court question through them without mistaking an intended workflow for a completed approval system.">
  <section aria-labelledby="not-a-vote">
    <h2 id="not-a-vote">A comment opens a question; it does not settle the model</h2>
    <p>
      A precise observation gives reviewers something to investigate. It may lead to
      better wording, a new distinction, a request for more evidence or a conclusion
      that the existing meaning should remain. A useful outcome can also be a clearly
      recorded disagreement. None requires treating the number of comments, reactions
      or agreeing AI agents as authority.
    </p>
    <p>
      The sequence below is a guide to the reasoning and handoffs, illustrated with
      fictional Harbour Court feedback. It is not a live tracking system, a promised
      response time or evidence that every stage is currently implemented on this
      website. The applicable candidate and working-group records determine standing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <figure class="modelling-figure" aria-labelledby="journey-caption">
    <figcaption id="journey-caption">An illustrative review journey, with distinct responsibilities</figcaption>
    <ModellingVisual kind="candidate-loop" />
    <ol class="modelling-steps">
      <li><h3>Observation · Participant</h3><p>“The report was corrected on 20 August, but the inspection occurred on 12 August. This definition appears to combine those dates.”</p></li>
      <li><h3>Interpretation · Facilitator and domain reviewers</h3><p>Identify the subjects and evidence. Ask whether the problem is wording, missing structure, a source conflict or a difference between contexts.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Proposed change · Modelling contributors</h3><p>Draft separate meanings for inspection occurrence and report issue. Include the multi-day inspection counterexample and show what would change.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Checks · Technical specialists and tools</h3><p>Examine whether the encoding and declared rules preserve the proposed distinction. A check does not establish that the inspection happened or approve the meaning.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Disposition · Responsible human authority</h3><p>Record the response, rationale, remaining questions and the candidate or decision affected. Promotion requires the applicable recorded authority.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </figure>

  <section aria-labelledby="responses">
    <h2 id="responses">What an understandable response should tell you</h2>
    <p>
      “Resolved” is not enough if you cannot see what changed. A useful response
      connects the original question to the reasoning and its effect. For our case,
      you should be able to distinguish these possible outcomes:
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt>A change is proposed</dt><dd>The draft now separates the dates. It still needs review, and the affected candidate version should be identified.</dd>
      <dt>More evidence is needed</dt><dd>The usual case is clear, but the treatment of inspections spanning several days remains open.</dd>
      <dt>Different contexts need different views</dt><dd>One consumer needs the event date and another the latest report issue date. The model can preserve both without redefining them as one thing.</dd>
      <dt>No change is proposed</dt><dd>The existing definition already distinguishes the subjects, or the suggested replacement would introduce a different error. Explain that reasoning and point to the relevant definition.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      These are explanatory outcomes, not the names of an implemented OPDA issue
      tracker or an adopted lifecycle state machine. The
      <a href="/semantic-modelling/method">method section</a> separates the required
      modelling discipline from wider proposed operating arrangements.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="checks">
    <h2 id="checks">Passing a check answers a bounded question</h2>
    <div class="modelling-comparison">
      <section aria-labelledby="technical-answer">
        <h3 id="technical-answer">A technical check can ask</h3>
        <p>Does this report description contain the issue date required by this declared profile? Is that value expressed in the required form? Does the example preserve the intended relationship?</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="human-answer">
        <h3 id="human-answer">Domain review still has to ask</h3>
        <p>Is this the right date to require for the stated purpose? Does the definition work in practice? What evidence supports the claim, and who can authorise the proposed change?</p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <p>
      A file can satisfy an encoded rule while containing an inaccurate statement.
      Equally, a useful observation can reveal that the encoded rule itself is wrong.
      Tools help locate inconsistencies; people must interpret their consequences.
      This teaching example is not a current candidate validation result.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="disagreement">
    <h2 id="disagreement">When there is no clean answer yet</h2>
    <p>
      Subdivision gives Harbour Court a harder question: what makes Flat 1 the same
      dwelling over time? An unchanged address cannot settle it, and a new identifier
      alone cannot explain it. Record the competing interpretations, the evidence
      needed and which responsibility owns the next review. Do not hide the problem
      by merging the dwelling, building and title record.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Why preserve dissent if most people prefer one answer?</summary>
      <p>A minority example may reveal a material exception or a different context. Recording it makes the consequence of the majority's preferred design visible and provides a reason to revisit the decision when evidence changes. That does not mean every objection prevents progress; it means unresolved meaning should not be represented as settled agreement.</p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="available">
    <h2 id="available">Current routes, proposed capabilities and authority</h2>
    <p>
      Today, the site provides candidate documentation and the member guide. Pages
      that expose comments support contextual feedback for authorised signed-in
      participants; working-group Teams threads support discussion under the
      <a href="/development/working-groups/member-guide/teams-and-discussions">current
      discussion guidance</a>. File intake follows its separate authorised route.
    </p>
    <p>
      Richer change views, expanded review facilities and the wider lifecycle
      arrangements are not made operational simply by describing them here.
      <a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0065">ADR-0065</a> and
      <a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0068">ADR-0068</a> record wider proposals. The
      <a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0067">Property Pack decision, ADR-0067</a>, separately
      records that component's authority boundary: candidate generation and technical
      conformance do not grant agents release authority.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you contribute, keep the reference to the original question and follow its
      existing discussion thread. Ask which candidate or decision the response
      affects. If access or the next step is unclear, use the
      <a href="/development/working-groups/member-guide">member guide</a>; do not
      create a parallel public thread containing private evidence.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/semantic-modelling/contribute/review-a-definition">Prepare your first review question →</a></p>
  </section>
</ModellingLayout>
